.TH CARVE 1
.SH NAME
carve \- edit with sam's language on patterns of tokens
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B carve
[
.B -l
.I lang
]
.I script
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Carve
runs a
.IR sam (1)
.I script
on each
.IR file ,
whose patterns are patterns of tokens, as
.IR sift (1)
gives them, in place of regular expressions.
It finds each file's syntax program by the rules of
.B $home/lib/syn
or
.BR /lib/syn ,
as
.I sift
does, and edits the file in place: it writes the new text to a temporary file beside it, with
its mode, and renames that over the file, so the file changes whole or not at all.
With no files,
.I carve
reads its standard input, whose language
.B -l
names, and writes the edited text to its standard output, as
.IR sed (1)
does.
The edits keep every comment and blank that they do not replace.
As sam does,
.I carve
drops each NUL from the text it reads, with a warning, and reads bad UTF-8 as U+FFFD.
.PP
The script holds sam's commands, one a line, with sam's addresses:
.TP
.BR , " " ; " " . " " $
.PD 0
.TP
.BI # n " " n
.TP
.BR + " " -
.TP
.BI / pattern / " " ? pattern ?
.PD
.PP
and these commands:
.TP
.BI a/ text / " " c/ text / " " i/ text /
.PD 0
.TP
.B d
.TP
.BI m " addr" " " t " addr"
.TP
.BI s/ pattern / text /
.TP
.BI x/ pattern / " cmd" " " y/ pattern / " cmd"
.TP
.BI g/ pattern / " cmd" " " v/ pattern / " cmd"
.TP
.B p
.TP
.B =
.TP
.BI | cmd " " < cmd " " > cmd
.TP
.BR { " " }
.PD
.PP
They mean what they mean in sam.
A pattern matches whole tokens: a match begins at the start of a token and ends at the end of one.
A search from a place begins at the first token that starts there or after it, and a command on a
range, such as
.B x
or
.BR s ,
sees only the tokens wholly inside the range.
Each command runs on the text from before it, and its changes take effect when it
ends, as in sam; they must come in order through the text and must not overlap.
Then
.I carve
has the syntax program lex the text again for the next command.
.PP
As in sam, dot starts at
.BR #0 ,
so a script that works on the whole text begins with
.BR , .
A group,
.B \e(
to
.BR \e) ,
captures what it matches, and so does a binding
.IB x : item\f1,
numbered with the groups in the order they open.
In the text of
.BR s ,
.B &
stands for the match,
.BI \e n\f1,
for
.I n
from 1 to 9, for the
.IR n th
capture,
.BI \e x
for the token bound to
.IR x ,
.B \en
for a newline and
.BI \e c
for any other character
.IR c .
A name in the text is the longest of the pattern's names that it begins with, so
.I carve
refuses a binding named
.BR n .
.BR A ,
.B c
and
.B i
take their text literally, as in sam.
.SH EXAMPLES
Make each increment by one an
.BR ++ :
.IP
.EX
carve -l c ',s/x:@ident = :x + 1/\ex++/g' <old.c >new.c
.EE
.PP
Swap the first two arguments of the calls of
.I memmove
whose arguments are single tokens, in place:
.IP
.EX
carve ',s/memmove ( x:. , y:. , z:. )/memmove(\ey, \ex, \ez)/g' *.c
.EE
.PP
Rename a variable everywhere but in comments and strings:
.IP
.EX
carve ',s/count/n/g' file.c
.EE
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/sift
and
.B /sys/src/libtok
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sam (1),
.IR sift (1),
.IR tok (2),
.IR syn (4)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
An error, such as a search that finds nothing or changes out of order, ends the script for that
file;
.I carve
writes nothing for it and goes on to the next.
The exit status is
.B errors
if any file failed.
.SH BUGS
.I Carve
differs from sam on purpose:
its patterns are of tokens, and a binding captures as a group does;
.B =
puts the file's name first when
.I carve
edits files, as acme's Edit does;
.BR p ,
.B =
and
.B >
write to standard output, before the text when
.I carve
reads standard input;
an empty match falls at the start of a token;
it parses the whole script before it reads a file, and an error leaves its file unwritten;
and it has no
.BR b ,
.BR B ,
.BR D ,
.BR e ,
.BR f ,
.BR k ,
.BR n ,
.BR q ,
.BR r ,
.BR u ,
.BR w ,
.BR X ,
.BR Y ,
.BR ! ,
.BR cd ,
or the addresses
.B '
and
.BR \&" ,
which an interactive carve will have.
